Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical debugging of startup-time issues caused by a sidecar container, including readiness ordering and configuration mounting verification, plus safe rollback procedures while maintaining latency targets. It emphasizes concrete kubectl usage and observable outcomes in a real-world production scenario.

Key Concepts
- Kubernetes rollout status and readiness probe verification
- Sidecar container impact on startup sequencing and latency
- ConfigMap and Secret mounting validation techniques
- Zero-downtime rollback strategies with latency constraints
- Production debugging workflow under load conditions

Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Node.js snippet for monitoring deployment status
const k8s = require('@kubernetes/client-node');
const kc = new k8s.KubeConfig();
kc.loadFromDefault();

const appsV1 = kc.makeApiClient(k8s.AppsV1Api);
const watch = new k8s.Watch(kc);

async function monitorRollout(namespace, deployment) {
  const stream = await watch.watch(`/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/${namespace}/deployments/${deployment}`,
    {}, // watch options
    (type, obj) => {
      if (obj.status.readyReplicas === obj.spec.replicas) {
        console.log('Rollout completed successfully');
      }
    });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to diagnose multi-tenant feature-flag issues in production using Kubernetes, Redis, and tracing. It probes understanding of cache stampede, per-tenant routing, rollout safety, and latency requirements.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant feature flags and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Redis keyspace examination and TTL semantics
- Distributed tracing for tenant-aware requests
- Latency SLO verification under load

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo canary rollout flow
if (flagEnabledForTenant(tenant)) {
  routeToTenantService(tenant);
} else {
  routeToFallback(tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 10k tenants with dynamic flag sets?
- What rollback criteria would you codify (latency, error rate, traffic shift)?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess real-world troubleshooting, instrumentation, and change-management skills under production-like pressure. It probes how a candidate reasons about observability, bottlenecks, and safe rollbacks in a live system.

## Key Concepts

- Observability: p95/p99 latency, error rate, queue depth, throughput
- Backpressure and rate limiting: bounded queues, controlled retries
- Idempotency and safety: ensure duplicates don’t break state
- Canary and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple rate limiter example (token bucket)
function createRateLimiter(tokensPerWindow, windowMs) {
  let tokens = tokensPerWindow; let t0 = Date.now();
  return function allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - t0;
    if (elapsed > windowMs) { t0 = now; tokens = tokensPerWindow; }
    if (tokens > 0) { tokens--; return true; }
    return false;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify a rollback safety threshold for the canary?
- Which traces, metrics, and tests would you add to prevent recurrence?

## Why This Is Asked
